WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.879
Film Courage: James, what would you say to
that person who just can’t get that first

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:04.279
project off of the ground?

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:06.640
James Kicklighter, Filmmaker: Well no one’s
asking for your film.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.830
Let’s start there.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.130
No one cares about you.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.680
No one wants to hear your film.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.980
No one wants to hear your story because they
don’t know you exist.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.900
I don’t mean that in a harsh way but they
don’t care about your film.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.320
And so if you want your first film to be made
unless you are independently wealthy which

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.070
I was not independently wealthy being a child
of teachers.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.480
I had to figure out how to do it.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.630
I had to figure out how to put money together.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.790
My first film was a short film called THE
CAR WASH with a dear friend of mine named

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.730
Edith Ivey who is still with us.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.460
She is almost 91 now.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:52.580
And she agreed to be in my film because she
liked my script and I figured out how to finance

00:00:52.580 --> 00:01:01.450
it by making high-school graduation DVDs with
a camcorder having a basic package for $20.00

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.730
where you would get just your get college
graduation on a DVD.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:10.900
But for $30.00 you could get an upgraded package
with the exclusive behind-the-scenes photo

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.760
gallery before graduation and your name printed
on a disc.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.930
80 percent of the people bought the deluxe
edition even though there’s not really a

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.500
big difference between the two.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.110
But then I was able to use that money to channel
it into making my first film.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.970
I was able to fly Edith to Savannah (to Statesboro).

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.890
I was able to pay for a hotel, do catering,
all of these things.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:46.130
I was still in college and I didn’t have
money but I made enough money to pay her SAG

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.560
student fee.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.280
I made enough money to do all of those other
things.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.830
And it was because I looked at things entrepreneurially
and I said well how can I do this one thing

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.950
to do this other thing.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.420
Because nobody cared about my film and nobody
cared about me.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.390
It’s like I’ve never made anything, who
cares?

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.580
You are in college, whatever.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.260
I made it happen for myself.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:17.010
Because I made that choice and because I cast
the right actor, because I took it to film

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:23.720
festivals, because I went to those film festivals
(all on my own dime) not because I’m independently

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.970
wealthy.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:33.569
Again, I was able to get to a point where
now people give me checks to make films and

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:36.360
it was because I took that initiative.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.490
So it’s not that you can’y make your film.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.510
It’s that you are choosing not to make it
because you are choosing not to take that

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:43.849
leap.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:49.030
There is no guarantee when you take that leap
that you will have that wonderful series of

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.420
events happen (none).

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.560
I was just lucky that people liked the film
that I made.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.670
And so the best thing you can do is write
the best damn script you can make, get the

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:06.239
best feedback from people you know, from people
who happen to work in the industry.

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:11.310
If there’s an online script service that
can review your script, whatever it is.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:17.830
Take the notes, take the feedback, don’t
reject it, embrace those things because you

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.599
probably don’t know what you’re doing
yet because you haven’t done it.

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:24.140
And then make it the best film you can make.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.750
Then take it to festivals, put it on the internet,
do whatever you have to do and then do it

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.030
again.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.810
And it’s going to still take you awhile
before people give you a check to make a film

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:40.580
unless you suddenly get 20 million hits on
your film on Youtube or if it wins the Jury

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.761
Prize at Sundance right?

00:03:41.761 --> 00:03:46.690
It’s very unlikely that you are suddenly
going to start getting checks and so you have

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:52.990
to keep doing what I did which is do something
else, use that money to make something and

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:57.750
over time and over time I start shifting from
having to spend my own money to spending other

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:58.750
people’s.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.050
But with that comes and entirely different
set of challenges…entirely.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:05.530
Film Courage: You keep saying lucky…can
I just interject here?

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.380
But you also sound very organized and very
disciplined.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.230
James: Well you have to be and but that’s
not a skill you are born with I don’t think.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.760
That’s a skill that you learn.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.859
But I do sincerely believe that there is an
element of luck.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:20.400
You are probably tired of hearing me say that
word.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.559
There is an element of luck.

00:04:21.559 --> 00:04:27.819
But of course there also is (and that is the
first thing) preparation, discipline, and

00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:34.620
hopefully some talent too because you’ve
got to figure out how to get to that point

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:40.669
where you want to be and if you are not prepared
or organized ready, when the day comes and

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:45.620
you are making two features at the same time,
you are not going to be ready to do it because

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.960
you don’t know how to get there.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.309
You don’t know how to structure and discipline
and deliver because it’s very different

00:04:54.309 --> 00:04:59.559
when you go from a point where well this is
$1,000 I raised of my money to make a short

00:04:59.559 --> 00:05:03.930
to now I’m spending hundreds of thousands
of dollars or millions of dollars of someone

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.490
else’s money that has to be made back because
it’s a business and it’s not personal

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.490
anymore.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.210
That’s that transition.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:16.339
So if you can’t make that transition of
discipline, no one else will ever give you

00:05:16.339 --> 00:05:20.750
another multi-million dollar check to make
a film ever again in your life, won’t happen.

